Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||04-10-2001||10:57 PM||||I really think that is the cause.<BR>Example (exagrated, of course):<P>Take your normal 12oz. soda can and squeeze it in on the sides. <BR>Now go to the sink and fill it with water. <BR>Next get your mothers measuring cup and pour the water out.<BR>Do you have 12ozs. of water?<BR> <BR>I think . . . . NOT!<P>OH, I'm on the other side of the fence and get 20+ MPG no mater of year, trans. or engine type. Compinations of mostly highway and city driving (80% vrs. 20% respectively).<BR>I'm just not driving like I'm hell bent for leather. <BR>I do run around 75MPH on the highway most of the time. <P>When it says 'E' on the gauge, how much fuel do you put in and how many miles?||||reg||1
Z||000008||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-10-2001||11:30 PM||||My gas mileage just took a nose dive in the last month or so, from 26 to 18.  <P>In the last few months I have replaced all the usual items in my '84 4cyl/automatic, but I did not do a valve job when I replaced my head gasket.  It all looked like it had been done recently and the car came with a reciept for head replacement about a year ago.  <P>The engine is clean, does not leak, has compression of 150psi in all 4 cyls., and does not consume more than a quart of oil in 3,000 miles.  <P>I had a dyno run done on my car and I got 68HP.  Yes, 68 teenie-weenie-my-little-ponies.  The dyno plot shows my car got richer as the rpms increased.  Horsepower was constant until 4,000 rpms and then it took a big dive.  I felt sad that my $100 dyno plot was a sad f*cking joke.<P>Finally, I bought a vacuum gauge to see what it had to say.<P>I found my engine was varying in idle vacuum immediately after being rev'd.  The readings indicated a sticking vavle.<P>I was in disbelief, as I have fixed EVERYTHING!  I decided to watch the fuel come out of the injector.  It was funny, as there were drips comming out and the throttle butterfly was covered with fuel when the engine was running. Yep, the car was running a little rich at idle.  <P>The problem:  Since idle vacuum is low and varies, the ECM thinks the engine is not at idle so it supplies more fuel to match the vacuum.  That is the purpose of the Map sensor.  So, in this way, a sticking valve can reduce fuel economy and performance.<P>Now the question is whether to rebuild or buy another duke.  Economics dictate an engine exchange.  At the same time, I am considering changing from automatic transmission to 4spd standard.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||BC-GT||04-10-2001||11:45 PM||||yeah, you would have to measure gallons/litres burned to miles/kilometers driven to get true mileage regardless of tank "size".||||reg||1
